From cec5a9c8192069a2c3efcfa6c0726fd8d2a6ccae Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MeeshaBear1 <nilehagen@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 21:16:42 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] Add the stale-state cost: a plug-in that changes the rule
 under a name

The two frozen tests read what a learner's state saves. Nothing reads what
that state costs when it is wrong: a learner that replays whatever it stored
under a task's name scores the same on both tests as one that checks first.

continual_arc.stale reads the difference through the plug-in interface, with
no change to a benchmark slot, record or measure.

StaleAliasPlugin: after the host's last benchmark instance it requests
auxiliary episodes in pairs. Both episodes of a pair are the same fresh
instance of a task the run never served. One is shown under the alias of a
task the learner met (swapped), the other under the task's own, never-seen
alias (control). The stale-state cost is the budget used on the swapped
episodes less the budget used on the controls, matched by task and instance
seed, with metrics.transfer's bootstrap interval over tasks. On a seen test
played at --width 1 every episode is reset_before, so each one meets the
learner as it stood at the end of the stream.

drift_schedule, DriftAliasPlugin, drift_cost: the same reading inside the
stream. A schedule is rebuilt so that chosen tasks' slots, from one visit on,
are slots of an unseen task, and the plug-in shows them under the alias of
the task they replaced. The control is the same schedule without the plug-in.
protocol_of lists the change as a deviation, so a drifted stream is never
labelled canonical; that is the one edit outside the new module.

Tests use three learners with no model (keeps nothing; drops what it kept at
one wrong answer; trusts what it kept) on a synthetic task source, and assert
0, one wrong attempt per episode, and 15 of 16 charges per episode.

Not run here: ruff (not installed on the machine that wrote this) and the
data-marked tests (no data was fetched).

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
