@@ -0,0 +1,130 @@
+# Continual-ARC: the stale-state cost
+
+## Why
+
+The two frozen tests read what a learner's state saves: the share of its reset's spend it no longer pays on tasks it
+met (the seen test) and on tasks it never met (the held-out test). Neither reads what that state costs when it is
+wrong. A learner that runs whatever it stored under a task's name, and a learner that checks what it stored against
+the instance in front of it, land on the same point of the plane, and they part on the day a rule changes under a
+name. In a working setting that day comes: a client's format changes, a project's convention is reversed. The paper
+leaves a mode in which the rules drift for later work.
+
+`continual_arc.stale` reads that cost through the plug-in interface. The environment serves and charges every
+instance as it always does, and no benchmark slot, record or measure changes.
+
+## What it measures
+
+After the last benchmark instance of a run, `StaleAliasPlugin` asks for auxiliary episodes in pairs. Both episodes of
+a pair are the same fresh instance of a task the run never served, drawn from the generators the stream draws from.
+
+| episode | what the learner is shown | what it has stored under that name |
+|---|---|---|
+| swapped | the instance under the alias of a task it met in the stream | the rule of another task, now wrong |
+| control | the same instance under the unseen task's own alias | nothing |
+
+The stale-state cost is the budget used on the swapped episodes less the budget used on the controls, in points of
+the budget, with a 95 percent interval from a bootstrap over tasks. It is positive when stored state costs the
+learner something. The figures come from `metrics.transfer`, with the control in the run's place and the swapped play
+in the reset's, so the number is read as the frozen tests' T is read.
+
+## Decisions
+
+| decision | choice | why |
+|---|---|---|
+| the changed rule | a whole task the run never served, from the stream's own source (`rearc` on `arc1`, `arcgen` on `arc2`), less every task of the host schedule | its instances are fresh and verified like the stream's, and no instance of it can have been met |
+| the control | the same task at the same instance seed under its own alias | the two episodes differ in the name alone, so the difference is what the name called up |
+| what the plug-in changes | the task id that the learner's alias is drawn from, in the observation the runner shows; no action and no feedback | the environment keeps the slot, the answer and the charge, and the record keeps the real task id with `arm`, `pair` and `alias_of` in its metadata |
+| what the learner can tell | nothing: the episode type, the plug-in's name and the metadata are left out of its view | a learner that saw `addon` could drop its memory for those episodes alone; a frozen test does not announce itself either |
+| when the episodes are served | after the host's last benchmark instance | nothing follows them, so they cannot reach a benchmark record |
+| the learner's state | as the host serves its own instances: every episode is marked `reset_before` when every slot of the host is | on a seen test played with `--width 1` the learner's `reset` restores the end of the stream, so every episode meets the same frozen learner; on a reset stream the learner is wiped and the cost is zero by construction |
+| order | the swapped episode first on even pairs, the control first on odd ones; each alias and each unseen task used once | on a plain stream the learner goes on learning, and the second episode of a pair has seen the instance; alternating spreads that over both arms |
+| size | one pair for every seen task, 25 on `arc1` (`pairs` asks for fewer) | the interval is over tasks, so more instances of one pair would add little |
+| seed | the host schedule's (`seed` overrides it): the unseen tasks, the pairing and the instance seeds are a function of it | a second run of the same learner serves the same episodes |
+
+## Running it
+
+On a stream, with the learner going on learning:
+
+```bash
+uv run continual-arc run --learner my_pkg.learners:MyLearner --schedule stream.json --out runs/my-stream \
+  --plugin continual_arc.stale:StaleAliasPlugin
+```
+
+On a frozen learner, which is the cleaner reading: play the seen test one instance at a time, under the stream's
+task names, with a learner whose `reset` restores its state at the end of the stream (the README's rule for a test
+played narrower than its width).
+
+```bash
+uv run continual-arc schedule make --config arc1 --seed 0 --test seen --width 1 --out seen-w1.json
+mkdir -p runs/my-stale && cp runs/my-stream/alias-salt runs/my-stale/
+uv run continual-arc run --learner my_pkg.learners:MyLearner --schedule seen-w1.json --out runs/my-stale \
+  --plugin continual_arc.stale:StaleAliasPlugin
+```
+
+The reading is in `summary.json` under `plugins.plugins.stale_alias`, and the episodes are in `records.jsonl` with
+`phase: "stale-alias"`. `--plugin-kwargs '{"pairs": 10}'` serves ten pairs, and `donor_tasks` names the unseen tasks
+for a source that has no generators of its own.
+
+## Reading the number
+
+| field | meaning |
+|---|---|
+| `stale_state_cost` | budget used on the swapped episodes less budget used on the controls, in points; `ci95` and `se` from the bootstrap over tasks |
+| `swapped_budget`, `control_budget` | the budget used on each arm, 100 times the mean charge over the cap |
+| `extra_cost` | the same difference per episode in charges: 1.0 is one wrong attempt or one demonstration |
+| `solved` | the share of each arm's episodes solved |
+| `n`, `tasks` | matched episodes and the tasks they cover |
+
+Three learners with no model, on the test suite's synthetic tasks at the default settings (`tests/test_stale.py`):
+
+| learner | swapped | control | stale-state cost | per episode |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| keeps nothing between instances | 6.25 | 6.25 | 0.00 | 0 |
+| runs the program it kept for the name, and at a wrong answer drops it and buys a demonstration | 12.50 | 6.25 | 6.25 | 1 wrong attempt |
+| answers from what it kept for the name until the instance fails | 100.00 | 6.25 | 93.75 | 15 charges |
+
+Read it beside two other numbers. The control's budget says how much room there was: a learner that fails the unseen
+tasks under any name spends the whole budget on both arms, and its zero says only that there was no room. The seen test's
+share says whether there was anything to go stale: a learner that kept nothing pays nothing here, and a zero beside
+a seen-test share near zero says nothing about trust.
+
+## A rule that changes inside the stream
+
+`drift_schedule` rebuilds a stream so that, for each chosen task, every slot from its visit `at_visit` on (counted
+from 0) is a slot of an unseen task, in the same place and at the same instance seed. `DriftAliasPlugin` shows those
+slots under the alias of the task they replaced, so the rule changes at that visit and stays changed. The control is
+the same schedule played without the plug-in, where the new rule arrives under a name of its own. `protocol_of` lists
+the change as a deviation, so a drifted stream is never read as canonical.
+
+```python
+from continual_arc.runner import load_run
+from continual_arc.schedule import Schedule
+from continual_arc.stale import drift_cost, drift_schedule
+
+drift_schedule(Schedule.load("stream.json"), at_visit=4).save("stream-drift4.json")
+# continual-arc run ... --schedule stream-drift4.json --out runs/drift --plugin continual_arc.stale:DriftAliasPlugin
+# continual-arc run ... --schedule stream-drift4.json --out runs/drift-control
+schedule, config, records = load_run("runs/drift")
+print(drift_cost(records, load_run("runs/drift-control")[2], schedule, config))
+```
+
+`drift_cost` is the same reading over the changed slots, with `by_visit`: the cost at each visit since the change. On
+the synthetic tasks the learner that repairs pays 6.25 points at visit 0 and nothing after, and the one that trusts
+pays 93.75 and then the whole budget at every later visit. This mode costs two runs of the stream, and its two runs
+are two histories: the learner in the control has kept one more name than the learner under the plug-in.
+
+## Limits
+
+* It tests trust in a stored mapping from a task's name to its rule. A learner that finds what it kept by the look
+  of the input, and not by the name, is shown nothing stale here and pays nothing, whatever it would pay elsewhere.
+* The rule changes all at once and to an unrelated rule. Nothing here reads a rule that shifts by degrees, a rule
+  that changes in part, or a change the learner is told about.
+* On a plain stream the learner is not frozen. The alternating order balances what an episode teaches its pair's
+  other half; it does not remove it. The seen test at `--width 1` does.
+* Plug-ins need one user, and auxiliary episodes need the grid environment: `Arc3Env` has no `insert_next`, so
+  there is no ARC-AGI-3 reading, and `StaleAliasPlugin` refuses that track.
+* The unseen tasks are public ARC tasks, as the stream's are. A model that recalls one from pretraining solves both
+  arms more cheaply, which narrows the room and leaves the difference matched.
+* With one pair per task the interval rests on 25 tasks on `arc1`. Report it with the number.
+* A run with `StaleAliasPlugin` attached keeps its `protocol` block, since its benchmark records are the ones the
+  run would have had without it; `meta.json` names the plug-in.
